From e5c235a118163f26d570bd3fa32e909a9bce650d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:18:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/10] D-51: the drawn Earth is the WGS-84 ellipsoid; the
 datum mapping retired
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The scene draws the Earth the flight is flown on (no display-only
conversion):

- render/earth-shape.ts: ellipsoidGeometry flattens the globe by 1/298.257
  along the pole; the launch scene's Earth and clouds (scene.ts) and the
  orbit view's globe use it; the atmosphere's rim shell is flattened too.
- The physical sky's scattering tables are a spherical shell: it is fed the
  camera at its height above WGS-84 on its radius, or at Plesetsk (16.9 km
  inside the old sphere) every ray at or below the horizon met the shell's
  ground and the sky went black.
- launchpad.ts places the complex at the WGS-84 pad (groundPositionEci's
  point), recovery.ts the landing zones, the drone ship, the sea and the
  steppe at their geodetic latitude and height; their ground is laid on the
  ellipsoid's normal (geodesy.ts geodeticEnu), so a terrain disc's rim
  kilometres out lies on the ellipsoid (the radial frame left it ±46 m off
  at 14 km at 51° N). The vehicle stands on the radius, as flown.
- render/datum.ts: the Vostok-only mapping onto the drawn sphere is gone;
  what stays is the landed sphere stood on the drawn ground, and
  `landReturn` (was onEllipsoid) for the steppe and the shadows.
- Heights shown or used by the camera, the sky, the audio, the debris list,
  the followed stage and the glow read geodeticHeight; the rotation credit
  row is the WGS-84 pad's speed. telemetry's debris rows: every height is
  above WGS-84 (`fine` keeps the tenth-of-a-kilometre rows of a return).
- tests/vostok-render.test.ts follows: the return is drawn where it flies,
  the steppe's fields lie on the WGS-84 ground to 5 cm.

